From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: vh265: a Dolby Vision FEL title must keep its enhancement layer

The Dolby Vision core asks the base-layer decoder to stop feeding the
enhancement layer whenever its metadata parser returns MEL for an RPU
("bypass MEL" in amdv.c). That request latches bypass_dvenl_enable, which drops
the decoder from DECODE_MODE_MULTI_DVBAL to plain streambase - and a streambase
base layer never reports HEVC_FIND_NEXT_DVEL_NAL, the only event that ever hands
the shared core to the enhancement decoder. The pair stops alternating
permanently and nothing clears the latch.

On a profile 7 FEL Blu-ray the verdict is wrong. The RPU's own el_type field
reads "FEL", and the enhancement layer carries 8-27KB per frame of residual
against 512-569 bytes for a genuinely minimal one. Measured on John Wick 3 on
the AM9 Pro, which the player opens as "profile 7, full enhancement layer":

    honoured:  dveldec ready 118462  scheduled 5     20.65fps, 165 stutters/65s
    ignored:   dveldec ready  40135  scheduled 1469  23.99fps, 0 stutters

Three events are enough to latch it, and it arrives in the first second of the
feature, so the whole film plays base-layer-only with a repeated frame roughly
every 400ms.

Ignore the request by default. When the core's verdict is right the cost is one
wasted decode of a residual-free layer, and the measurement above is the
expensive case holding full rate, so the headroom is there. Clear
dv_ignore_bypass_el to get the stock behaviour back.
---
--- a/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
+++ b/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
@@ -571,6 +571,12 @@
 static u32 dv_debug;
 
 static u32 force_bypass_dvenl;
+/*
+ * Ignore the Dolby Vision core's request to stop feeding the enhancement
+ * layer. See the event handler for why that request is wrong on a profile 7
+ * FEL Blu-ray. On by default; clear it to get the stock behaviour back.
+ */
+static u32 dv_ignore_bypass_el = 1;
 #endif
 #endif
 
@@ -9853,9 +9859,45 @@
 	else if (type & VFRAME_EVENT_RECEIVER_DOLBY_BYPASS_EL) {
 		if ((force_bypass_dvenl & 0x80000000) == 0) {
 			hevc_print(hevc, 0,
-			"%s: VFRAME_EVENT_RECEIVER_DOLBY_BYPASS_EL\n",
-			__func__);
-			hevc->bypass_dvenl_enable = 1;
+			"%s: VFRAME_EVENT_RECEIVER_DOLBY_BYPASS_EL%s\n",
+			__func__, dv_ignore_bypass_el ? " (ignored)" : "");
+			/*
+			 * The Dolby Vision core sends this - "bypass MEL" in
+			 * amdv.c - when its metadata parser returns MEL for an
+			 * RPU. A minimum enhancement layer carries no residual,
+			 * so there is nothing to gain from decoding it and the
+			 * base layer may as well run alone.
+			 *
+			 * Honouring it is not a cheap no-op, though: it latches
+			 * bypass_dvenl_enable, which drops this decoder from
+			 * DECODE_MODE_MULTI_DVBAL to plain streambase - and a
+			 * streambase base layer never reports
+			 * HEVC_FIND_NEXT_DVEL_NAL, which is the ONLY thing that
+			 * ever hands the shared core to the enhancement
+			 * decoder. The pair stops alternating for good, and
+			 * nothing clears the latch.
+			 *
+			 * On a profile 7 FEL title that verdict is wrong. The
+			 * RPU's own el_type field reads "FEL" (libdovi, in the
+			 * player) and the enhancement layer carries 8-27KB per
+			 * frame of residual, against 512-569 bytes for a
+			 * genuinely minimal one. Measured on John Wick 3, which
+			 * the player opens as "profile 7, full enhancement
+			 * layer": the event arrives in the first second of the
+			 * feature and from then on the enhancement decoder is
+			 * ready 118462 times and scheduled 5. The base layer
+			 * plays alone at 20.7fps with a repeated frame every
+			 * ~400ms - 165 of them in 65 seconds. Ignoring it, the
+			 * layers stay in lockstep (1402 frames each) at
+			 * 23.99fps with no repeated frame at all.
+			 *
+			 * When the verdict IS right the cost is one wasted
+			 * decode of a residual-free layer, and there is room
+			 * for it: the FEL case above is the expensive one and
+			 * it holds full rate.
+			 */
+			if (!dv_ignore_bypass_el)
+				hevc->bypass_dvenl_enable = 1;
 		}
 	}
 #endif
@@ -16963,6 +17005,9 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(dv_debug, "\n dv_debug\n");
 
 module_param(force_bypass_dvenl, uint, 0664);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(dv_ignore_bypass_el,
+	"\n ignore the dv core's bypass-el request (wrong on profile 7 FEL)\n");
+module_param(dv_ignore_bypass_el, uint, 0664);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(force_bypass_dvenl, "\n force_bypass_dvenl\n");
 #endif
 
